tag_args points into a mutable buffer; stop calling it const.

WHY THE BUILD FAILS:

    ../tag.c:564:29: error: assignment discards 'const' qualifier from
                     pointer target type [-Werror=discarded-qualifiers]
    cc1: all warnings being treated as errors

Line 564 is `point = strchr(tag_args[i], '.')` assigning to `char *point`.
tag_args is declared `const char *[]`, so under C23 glibc's const-generic
strchr returns `const char *` and the assignment drops the qualifier. yambar
builds with -Werror, so a warning is fatal.

-std=c18 IS ALREADY REQUESTED AND DOES NOT HELP, which is the interesting part
and different from every other C23 casualty in this set. yambar's meson.build
sets c_std=c18, and the failing compile line shows `-std=c18`. glibc's
string.h:120 gates the const-generic macro on

    __GLIBC_USE (ISOC23) && defined __glibc_const_generic && !defined _LIBC

and __GLIBC_USE(ISOC23) is not driven by -std alone. So pinning the language
version -- which fixed gmp and unifdef -- cannot fix this one.

THE const WAS WRONG, AND THE CODE ALREADY SAID SO. tag_args[i] is assigned
from strtok_r(tag_name_and_arg, ...), which returns a pointer INTO A MUTABLE
BUFFER, and line 565 immediately writes through it:

    *point = '\0';

So the array was declared const and then mutated. Dropping const makes the
declaration describe what the code does, rather than casting the qualifier away
at the one line that trips the compiler. Every other use of tag_args -- 14
strcmp calls, is_number, an index, a LOG_WARN -- takes const char * and accepts
a char * unchanged.

WHY NOT -Wno-discarded-qualifiers OR -Wno-error. Both would silence this
instance and every future one, in a package that compiles with -Werror
deliberately. The fault is two lines of over-restrictive typing, not the
warning.

THIRD strchr CASUALTY. wlroots hit the same glibc change at xcursor.c:605 and
carries patches/0002 for it; that one is fixed by widening the variable to
const, because wlroots only READS through the result. Here the opposite
direction is correct, because yambar writes.

Generated with `diff -u`, not written by hand.

